  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>For numbers 1 – 3, use the following situation:</para>
      <para>Andre’s coin jar contains 85 cents. There are no quarters or pennies in the jar, so the
        jar has all nickels, all dimes, or some of each.</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          <para>Write an equation that relates the number of nickels, \( n \), the number of dimes,
            \( d \), and the amount of money, in cents, in the coin jar.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE --><para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(5n+10d=85\), where n is the number of nickels and d is the number of dimes.</para>
          <para>\(5n+10d=85\), where n is the number of nickels and d is the number of dimes.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--START
      QUESTION 2 -->

        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <para>Graph your equation on the coordinate plane. Be sure to label the axes. Use the
              graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents
              this scenario using the Desmos tool below.</para>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <para>When you have finished graphing, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
              button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <figure class="unnumbered">
              <media
                alt="GRAPH OF A LINE WITH \(y\)-intercepts OF 8.5 AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (1, 8) AND (17, 0).">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_01_12_06_01.jpg" />
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><exercise>
        <problem>
          <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          <para>How many nickels are in the jar if there are no dimes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para>Compare your answer:</para> 17 nickels
          17 nickels </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          <para>How many dimes are in the jar if there are no nickels?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para>Compare your answer:</para> It's not
          possible for the coin jar to have no nickels if the amount of money in it is 85 cents.
          It's not possible for the coin jar to have no nickels if the amount of money in it is 85
          cents. </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem> 
          <para>What are all the different ways the coin jar could have 85 cents if it could also
            contain quarters?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution><para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> Listed as
          nickels, dimes, quarters: (17,0,0), (15,1,0), (13,2,0), (12,0,1), (11,3,0), (10,1,1),
          (9,4,0), (8,2,1), (7,0,2), (7,5,0), (6,3,1), (5,1,2),(5,6,0), (4,4,1), (3,2,2), (2,0,3),
          (3,7,0), (2,5,1), (1,3,2), (0,1,3), (1,8,0), (0,6,1)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Self Check</title>
      <para>A coin jar contains 75 cents and only contains quarters and
        nickels. Write an equation that relates the number of quarters, \( q \), the number of
        nickels, \( n \), and the amount of money, in cents, in the coin jar.</para>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section>
        <title>Standard Form of Linear Equation</title>
  
        <para>A linear equation is in <emphasis>standard form</emphasis> when it is written
          \(Ax+By=C\).</para>
        <para>Most people prefer to have \(A\), \(B\), and \(C\) be integers and \(A\ge 0\) when
          writing a linear equation in standard form, although it is not strictly necessary.</para>
        <list>
          <item>Linear equations have infinitely many solutions.</item>
          <item>For every number that is substituted for \(x\) there is a corresponding \(y\) value.</item>
          <item>This pair of values is a solution to the linear equation and is represented by the
            ordered pair \((x,y)\).</item>
          <item>When we substitute these values of \(x\) and \(y\) into the equation, the result is a
            true statement, because the value on the left side is equal to the value on the right
            side.</item>
        </list>
      </section>
      
      <section>
        <title>Solution of a Linear Equation in Two Variables</title>
  
        <para>An ordered pair \((x,y)\) is a solution of the linear equation \(Ax+By=C\), if the
          equation is a true statement when the \(x\)– and \(y\)–values of the ordered pair are
          substituted into the equation.</para>
        <para>Linear equations have infinitely many solutions. We can plot these solutions in the
          rectangular coordinate system. The points will line up perfectly in a straight line. We
          connect the points with a straight line to get the graph of the equation. We put arrows on
          the ends of each side of the line to indicate that the line continues in both directions.</para>
        <para>A graph is a visual representation of all the solutions of the equation. It is an
          example of the saying, “A picture is worth a thousand words.” The line shows you <emphasis
            effect="italics">all</emphasis> the solutions to that equation. Every point on the
          line is a solution of the equation. And, every solution of this equation is on this line.
          This line is called the graph of the equation. Points <emphasis effect="italics">not</emphasis>
          on the line are not solutions!</para>
        <para>When a linear equation in standard form is solved for \(y\), it is then easy to pick out
          the slope of the line and where the graph of the line crosses the \(y\)–axis.</para>

          <section>
        <title>Example:</title>
        <para>Solve \( 9x - 3y = 15 \) for y</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1 -<emphasis>Move the \(x\) term to isolate the \(y\) term.</para>
        <para>Subtract \(9x\) from both sides.</para>
        <para>\( 9x - 9x - 3y = 15 - 9x \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis>Simplify.</para>
        <para>\( -3y = 15 - 9x \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis>Divide by the coefficient of \(y\).</para>
        <para>Divide both sides by \(-3\)</para>
        <para>\( \frac{-3y}{-3} = \frac{15-9x}{-3} \)</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis>Simplify.</para>
        <para>\( y = 3x - 5 \)</para>
        <para>In the new equation, \( y = 3x - 5 \), the slope is \(3\) and the graph of the line
          would cross the \(y\)–axis at -5.</para>
        </section>

        <note class="try look-at-me">
        <title>Try It: Understanding Standard Form</title>
  
        <!--Q#-->
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Solve \( 6x + 2y =14 \) for \( y \).</para>
  
          </problem>
          <solution>
  
            
  
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
            <para><emphasis>Step 1 -<emphasis>Move the \(x\) term to isolate the \(y\) term.</para>
  
            <para>Subtract \(6x\) from both sides.</para>
  
            <para>\( 6x - 6x + 2y = 14 - 6x \)</para>
  
            <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis>Simplify.</para>
  
            <para>\(2y = -6x +14\)</para>
  
            <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis>Divide by the coefficient of \(y\).</para>
  
            <para>Divide both sides by 2.</para>
  
            <para>\( \frac{2y}{2} = \frac{-6x+14}{2} \)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis>Simplify.</para>
  
            <para>\( y = -3x + 7 \)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
      </section>
    </section>
